{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"answers\":[{\"question_id\":\"Q015_artifact_tag_not_homology\",\"answer\":\"The second segment, d1a14h2 (1a14 H:1–8; sunid 343393), is a synthetic N-terminal tag. SCOPe 2.08 places it in l.1.1.1: family “Tags,” superfamily “Tags,” fold “Tags,” class “Artifacts.” These are artifact bookkeeping categories, not evidence of an evolutionarily related protein family or a genuine autonomous fold.\\n\\nSimilarity to this short tag should not be treated as evidence of natural protein homology: it can reflect a shared engineered sequence or chance similarity. Exclude or mask it when inferring homology, transferring function, or choosing natural-domain representatives and structural templates. It does not justify transferring immunoglobulin identity or antigen-binding function to another protein.\\n\\nThe biological domain is d1a14h1 (H:9–111; sunid 20061), an immunoglobulin heavy-chain variable domain in the V-set family (b.1.1.1), immunoglobulin superfamily (b.1.1), immunoglobulin-like beta-sandwich fold (b.1), and all-beta class (b). Its family and superfamily placement support evolutionary relationships; fold or class similarity alone does not establish homology. Even genuine variable-domain homology does not establish identical antigen specificity.\",\"evidence_used\":[\"/api/v1/\",\"/api/v1/releases/2.08/resolve/d1a14h2\",\"/api/v1/releases/2.08/resolve/d1a14h1\",\"/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/343393\",\"/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/20061\",\"/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/311501\",\"/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/88552\",\"/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/310894\",\"/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/88543\",\"/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/310682\",\"/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/48727\",\"/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/310607\",\"/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/48726\",\"/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/310573\",\"/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/48725\",\"/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/310555\",\"/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/48724\"]}]}"}}
